            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your patient, a 23-year-old male, admitted on 21/04/2014 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically consistent with acute appendicitis. On admission, physical examination revealed rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Blood tests showed leukocytosis. Abdominal ultrasound confirmed appendiceal inflammation without perforation. The patient underwent laparoscopic emergency appendectomy on 22/04/2014 without intraoperative complications. Postoperatively, intravenous antibiotics and analgesics were administered, and wound care was provided. Vital signs remained stable throughout the stay. The patient recovered well and was discharged on 25/04/2014 with oral analgesia and wound care instructions. Please arrange follow-up in approximately two weeks for wound inspection and histology results review.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
